<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The JobBridge mark, as a status-bar notification icon (§9.2).

  ## Why this file has to exist

  Without `default_notification_icon` in AndroidManifest.xml, FCM falls back to
  the application icon - and since API 21 Android treats a notification's small
  icon as an **alpha mask**: every non-transparent pixel is painted in the
  system's tint. `@mipmap/ic_launcher` is a full-bleed navy plate, entirely
  opaque, so the fallback renders as a **solid white square** on every
  notification this product ever sends.

  ## Why both figures are one colour here, which the design calls a misuse

  They are not: the platform discards colour outright. `#FFFFFFFF` below is
  never drawn - only the alpha is read - so there is no "both figures
  turquoise" to commit. The two-tone mark is unrepresentable in a status bar on
  any Android version this app supports, and choosing a ground (HhBrandMark's
  rule) has nothing to act on. The colour is white rather than navy so that
  anything previewing this file flat, outside a notification, still shows the
  silhouette the platform will draw.

  ## The geometry

  Same path data as `ic_launcher_foreground.xml` and the design document,
  byte-identical so all three can be diffed against each other. Only the
  enclosing transform differs, because the constraint differs: an adaptive
  foreground is masked and takes 48% of its layer, and a notification icon is
  **not masked at all**, so it takes the ~2dp of padding Android's own
  specification asks for and fills the rest.

    mark width  = 0.85 x 24 = 20.4          (2dp clear on the long axis, ~7.5%)
    mark height = 20.4 x 19.8 / 23 = 17.5617   (aspect is locked at 23 : 19.8)
    outer group = scale 0.886957 (= 20.4 / 23), translate (1.8, 3.21915)
    inner group = translate (-4.5, -6.2), the designer's viewBox origin

  **Not the single-figure form.** HhBrandMark drops to one figure below 20pt,
  and 24dp is under that - but that rule is about legibility of the *two-tone*
  mark at small sizes on screen, where the figures must stay distinguishable
  from each other. A silhouette has no such problem: the two shapes read as one
  form, which is the whole idea the mark carries.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <group
        android:scaleX="0.886957"
        android:scaleY="0.886957"
        android:translateX="1.8"
        android:translateY="3.21915">
        <group
            android:translateX="-4.5"
            android:translateY="-6.2">

            <!-- Left figure: the candidate. -->
            <path
                android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
                android:pathData="M5,9.8a3.6,3.6 0 1,0 7.2,0a3.6,3.6 0 1,0 -7.2,0" />
            <path
                android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
                android:pathData="M4.5 26C4.5 18.5 5.8 13.2 8.6 13.2C11.5 13.2 13.4 12.4 15.2 11.8L15.2 17.5C12.5 18.2 10.5 20.5 10.5 26Z" />

            <!-- Right figure: the employer, reaching toward the candidate. -->
            <path
                android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
                android:pathData="M19.8,9.8a3.6,3.6 0 1,0 7.2,0a3.6,3.6 0 1,0 -7.2,0" />
            <path
                android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
                android:pathData="M27.5 26C27.5 18.5 26.2 13.2 23.4 13.2C20.5 13.2 18.6 12.4 16.8 11.8L16.8 17.5C19.5 18.2 21.5 20.5 21.5 26Z" />
        </group>
    </group>
</vector>
